findings.meaning:
Runs once per reaction (inside phase 0 of the generic stagger, which then sets +0x6=1):
  ent[0x223] -= dec[ent[0x5]];                 // signed byte, ALWAYS stored (delay slot 0x801055ec)
  if (ent[0x10E] & 0x40) skip reseed;
  if ((int8)ent[0x223] > 0) skip reseed;
  if ((s16)ent[0x156] < 81) skip reseed;       // HP < 81 -> leave the cooldown expired (stays knockdown-prone)
  ent[0x223] = rand()&0xF + 16;
dec[] @0x8010CC33 indexed by routine_1: [1]=0x09, [2]=0x0F, [3]=0x0F, [4]=0x23, [5..12]=0x00, [13]=0x14, [14..16]=0x00. Index 0 reads 0x80 (the top byte of the pointer 0x80107888 at 0x8010CC30) — an intentional/incidental overlap; reaction id 0 never occurs.
So weak hits (id 1) erode 9 per hit off a 16..31 pool = 2..4 hits to enable a knockdown; id 4 erodes 35 = instant.
All other seed sites use the SAME random 16..31 shape: 0x8010089c, 0x80102d48, 0x80104034, 0x801045e8, 0x80104a18, 0x801050c8, 0x80105620 (all `andi 0xf` + `addiu 16`); 0x8010094c uses `andi 0xf` + `addiu 32`; 0x80106030 uses `andi 0x10` + `addiu 15`.

546 = 0x222. Once the cooldown gate passes:
  if (ent[0x222] == 1 || ent[0x5] == 1) {   // already latched, OR weakest reaction id
      ent[0x222] = 1;
      ent[4..7] = 0x00000501;               // -> state 1 (ACTIVE), routine_1 = 5  == LIGHT FLINCH
      ent[0x223] = rand()&0xF + 16;
      spawn blood; RETURN (the HURT executor never runs this frame)
  } else {
      ent[0x222] = 1;                        // 0x80105164 (v0 == 1 from the 0x8010509c delay slot)
      fall into the body
  }
Then 0x80105438 checks ent[0x222]==1 FIRST and tail-calls 0x80105bc0 (the knockdown) instead of the stagger. So:
  ent[0x222]==0 -> stagger 0x80105438
  ent[0x222]==1 -> knockdown 0x80105bc0
Reaction id 1 (the weakest attack class) can therefore NEVER produce a knockdown — it always takes the ACTIVE-substate-5 shortcut. ACTIVE substate 5 has a STUB decision (0x80103180: `jr ra`) and a real executor (0x80103188), i.e. it is a non-decision-making scripted flinch.

Both exits use FUN_80015614(ent, player.x@0x800CFC30, player.z@0x800CFC38, cone) — a bearing/cone test built on FUN_800154AC.
STAGGER exit (routine words are `sw` into ent+4, so they set state+routine_1 together):
  if (!cone(512) && dist<0xFA0 && !(0x800CFDCB & 0x80) && (rand&0xF)==0) -> 0x0B01 (ACTIVE, routine_1 = 11)
  else if (cone(1024) && (rand&1))                                      -> 0x0C01 (ACTIVE, routine_1 = 12)
  else if (rand&1)                                                      -> 0x0201 (ACTIVE, routine_1 = 2)
  else { ent[4] = 257 (0x0101 -> WALK); if (ent[0x218] == ent[0x14C]) ent[4] = 0x00010101 and randomize gait ent[0x16B]=(rand&0xF)*2, ent[0x158]=30, ent[0x16A]=(rand&0x1F)+30, ent[0x15A]=rand+300 }
  Note the DELAY SLOT at 0x80105b40: `sw v0,4(s1)` stores 257 unconditionally before the `bne` decides.
KNOCKDOWN exit (0x8010603c-0x801060f8) is the same shape but with a rand&0x3 gate for 0x0B01 and an extra dist<0xBB8 + cone(900) test for 0x0C01.
So a RE2 zombie can come out of a hit reaction directly into an attack substate, not necessarily back into WALK.

This runs in PHASE 2 of the stagger (the unbend phase, ent[0x6]==2), i.e. while the zombie is still visibly reacting. s3 here is ent[0x1F0] (the distance to the player, loaded at 0x8010545c and NOT overwritten on this path).
  if (dist < 0x514 /*1300*/ && !(0x800CFDCB & 0x80) && ent[0x106] == [0x800CFCFE] && !([0x800CFE18][0] & 1)) {
      if (!(ent[0x21A] & 0x20) && FUN_80015758(&ent[0x38], 0x800CFE65, ent.yaw+256, 288) == 0) ent[4] = 769;  // 0x0301 -> ACTIVE routine_1 = 3
      if (!(ent[0x21A] & 0x40) && FUN_80015758(&ent[0x38], 0x800CFC30, ent.yaw-256, 288) == 0) ent[4] = 769;
  }
The two ent[0x21A] guards (0x20 / 0x40) are exactly the two ARM-BLOWN-OFF latches from the dismemberment finding: a zombie whose left arm is gone cannot reach with that arm, and the reach test is offset +256/-256 (22.5 deg) to each side. That is a direct mechanical coupling between dismemberment and grab capability.
